      'A management company pools money from investors to create a portfolio of securities (a fund). An investment adviser (fund manager) is hired by the fund to make investment decisions based on the fund\'s investment objective. There are two types of management companies: open end and closed end.\n\nOpen-End Management Companies (Mutual Funds)\n\nOpen-end management companies are commonly known as mutual funds. Mutual funds can only issue common shares. The number of shares that can be issued is unlimited (which is where the term "open end" comes from).\n\nAs investors buy shares from the fund, new shares are issued, but unlike shares of common stock, these shares are nonnegotiable. This means they cannot be traded. Instead, investors sell their shares back to the fund, and the fund redeems (expires) them.\n\nClosed-End Management Company (Closed-End Funds)\n\nA closed-end fund has a one-time issuance of stock. These securities are not redeemable. Instead, they trade like any other negotiable securities, usually on an exchange.\n\nUnlike open-end funds, closed-end funds may raise money to run their business by issuing common stock, preferred stock, or debt. Note: Don\'t confuse the capitalization of a fund (the raising of money to invest) with the fund\'s portfolio of investments.',
