      'A unit investment trust issues shares of beneficial interest representing an undivided interest in a unit of the trust\'s portfolio. There are two types of UlTs-fixed trusts and non-fixed trusts. There are two types of UITs-fixed trusts and non-fixed trusts.\n\nFixed UIT\n\nA fixed trust selects a portfolio of securities (usually bonds). Once the portfolio is selected, it is not changed: no buying or selling takes place in the trust. There is no management of the trust and therefore no management fee.\n\nThe trust then sells units to investors. As interest payments are made on the bonds, they are passed to the unit holders. As bonds mature, the principal is repaid to the unit holders.\n\nWhen all the bonds have matured, the trust selfliquidates.\n\nNon-Fixed UIT\n\nNon-fixed trusts do not invest directly in stocks or bonds but instead invest in mutual fund shares. Non-fixed trusts are used within insurance company products such as variable annuities or variable life insurance contracts.\n\nVariable Annuity\n\nVariable annuities are not considered to be investment companies, but they do use investment companies to provide investment options to their customers. Annuities are generally used as retirement planning products. Individuals purchase units in a separate account (often a UIT) to fund their future retirement needs.',
